<Lab title="Catch the incident" area="AIOps" lede="An alert asks a question someone thought of in advance. A detector looks for anything unusual, and it has to be scored honestly. Here the incidents are injected, so the right answers are known.">
  <AnomalyLab client:load />
  <h2>Things to try</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Start with mean and SD,</strong> at a score of 5. It misses incidents, because spikes in the warm-up stretched the standard deviation. Select "Another day" a few times, and watch the result swing with whatever the warm-up happened to contain.</li>
    <li><strong>Switch to median and MAD.</strong> At the same score it finds all three, day after day. A few spikes barely move a median.</li>
    <li><strong>Drop that threshold to 2.</strong> Count the false alarms you paid for: often more than a dozen in a day. An on-call team soon stops trusting a noisy detector.</li>
    <li><strong>Try EWMA.</strong> It catches the sharp jump, then learns the creep and the plateau as normal, because it never stops learning. That's what lets it follow a busy afternoon. Lower its threshold and it finds most of them, but look at how little of each incident stays under alarm.</li>
    <li><strong>Raise "minutes in a row".</strong> Lone spikes stop firing, and every real detection arrives later.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Go deeper</h2>
